.TH MASTO 1
.SH NAME
masto \- Mastodon client: print the home timeline
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B masto
.I credfile
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B Masto
fetches the caller's home timeline from a Mastodon instance using the
instance hostname and personal access token read from
.IR credfile ,
then prints it, one line per status, oldest last:
.EX
\&@alice@mastodon.social: what a nice day
\&@bob@example.social: shipped a thing
.EE
.PP
.I Credfile
holds exactly two lines: the instance hostname with no scheme
(\c
.B mastodon.social
, not
.BR https://mastodon.social ),
then a personal access token \(em from that instance's own Settings >
Development > New Application page, with
.B read
scope. This skips Mastodon's OAuth2 authorization-code exchange
entirely: a personal access token is a real, first-class credential
Mastodon itself issues for exactly this case, not a workaround. Keep
this file readable only by its owner; nothing here enforces that.
.PP
.B Masto
never places the token on a command line (where a
.IR ps (1)
listing could see it) or in any diagnostic output.
.PP
Status text arrives from Mastodon as a small fragment of HTML
(typically wrapped in
.BR <p> ,
with
.B <br>
for line breaks and
.B <a>
/
.B <span>
around mentions and hashtags); this strips tags, treating
.B <br>
and
.B </p>
as line separators rather than dropping them outright (which would
otherwise glue adjacent words together with no space between them),
and decodes the handful of entities Mastodon's own renderer emits
.RI ( &amp; ,
.IR &lt; ,
.IR &gt; ,
.IR &quot; ,
.IR &apos; / &#39; )
rather than pull in a real HTML parser for a few paragraphs of text.
.SH TRANSPORT
Same as
.IR bsky (1):
this tree's own
.B ssl3
cannot reach a real TLS 1.2+ host, so
.B masto
drives the host's own
.IR curl (1)
through
.B #C
(see
.IR cmd (3)).
Hosted mode only \(em see
.IR bsky (1)
for the full reasoning, not repeated here.
.SH SOURCE
.B /appl/cmd/masto.b
.SH SEE ALSO
.IR mastotest (1),
.IR bsky (1),
.IR cmd (3)
.SH BUGS
Read-only, one timeline, no abstraction shared with
.IR bsky (1)
(each is deliberately self-contained \(em see masto.b's own header
comment for why). No pagination (a fixed 30 statuses), no posting, no
notifications, no boosts or favourites shown separately from the
statuses that carry them. The HTML stripper is a linear tag scan, not
a real parser, though it does track quoted attribute values so a
literal
.B >
inside one does not end the tag early.
